WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.381
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.381 --> 00:00:06.134
write and restore a binary record, a group of

00:00:06.134 --> 00:00:08.949
stored values whose types, order, and meanings

00:00:08.949 --> 00:00:10.540
follow a shared agreement.

00:00:11.189 --> 00:00:14.326
Typed binary input and output help applications

00:00:14.326 --> 00:00:17.397
exchange saved values reliably, while a shared

00:00:17.397 --> 00:00:20.400
field agreement lets the reading code recover

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.604
what those values mean for a booking, a setting,

00:00:23.604 --> 00:00:24.739
or a game record.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.461
A campus event desk has four seats available at

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:31.131
two dollars and fifty cents per seat, and

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:32.173
booking is open.

00:00:32.173 --> 00:00:35.168
We will save that offer in a private temporary

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:38.294
file, then restore its count, price, and booking

00:00:38.294 --> 00:00:38.750
status.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.302
The recovered report should show four seats, a

00:00:42.302 --> 00:00:44.951
price of two point five, and true for open

00:00:44.951 --> 00:00:45.393
status.

00:00:45.393 --> 00:00:48.358
The writer and reader must agree on an integer,

00:00:48.358 --> 00:00:50.818
a double, and a boolean, in that order.

00:00:50.818 --> 00:00:53.279
After reading, we will remove only this

00:00:53.279 --> 00:00:54.225
example's file.

00:00:54.902 --> 00:00:57.524
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.902
Main.java and build the record program.

00:01:03.735 --> 00:01:06.813
We need the two data-stream types for primitive

00:01:06.813 --> 00:01:09.432
values, plus Files and Path for the file

00:01:09.432 --> 00:01:10.022
location.

00:01:10.022 --> 00:01:12.772
Main is the class containing this program.

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:15.719
Its main method is where execution begins; we

00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:18.535
will place the record operations inside it.

00:01:36.066 --> 00:01:38.803
The imports let us use those short library

00:01:38.803 --> 00:01:39.194
names.

00:01:39.194 --> 00:01:41.997
The throws declaration allows a checked I/O

00:01:41.997 --> 00:01:44.213
failure to leave this main method.

00:01:44.213 --> 00:01:46.885
It does not catch the failure or create a

00:01:46.885 --> 00:01:47.863
default record.

00:01:47.863 --> 00:01:50.991
Next, we need one owned file for the two phases.

00:01:58.408 --> 00:02:01.401
CreateTempFile makes a new empty temporary file

00:02:01.401 --> 00:02:02.739
and returns its Path.

00:02:02.739 --> 00:02:05.604
The prefix helps identify the name, generated

00:02:05.604 --> 00:02:08.661
characters distinguish it, and the suffix is dot

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:08.916
bin.

00:02:08.916 --> 00:02:11.209
The suffix does not choose a format.

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:14.265
We keep the returned path so the writer, reader,

00:02:14.265 --> 00:02:16.558
and final cleanup use the same file.

00:02:16.572 --> 00:02:19.594
First, open byte output and wrap it with typed

00:02:19.594 --> 00:02:20.842
writing operations.

00:02:20.842 --> 00:02:23.798
Then write the seat count, price, and booking

00:02:23.798 --> 00:02:25.572
flag in their agreed order.

00:02:48.287 --> 00:02:50.684
The Files call opens the byte stream.

00:02:50.684 --> 00:02:53.730
DataOutputStream adds operations that represent

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.155
primitive Java values.

00:02:55.155 --> 00:02:58.006
Each write adds its value after the previous

00:02:58.006 --> 00:02:58.266
one.

00:02:58.266 --> 00:03:01.246
We stored the values, not their variable names

00:03:01.246 --> 00:03:04.162
or the labels that will appear in the report.

00:03:04.182 --> 00:03:07.069
The writer closes as its resource block ends,

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:09.442
including its underlying byte stream.

00:03:09.442 --> 00:03:12.008
We can now open a separate reader at the

00:03:12.008 --> 00:03:14.895
beginning and recover the same three types in

00:03:14.895 --> 00:03:15.857
the same order.

00:03:37.241 --> 00:03:40.095
The first read receives the integer seat count.

00:03:40.095 --> 00:03:43.009
The next receives the double price, and the last

00:03:43.009 --> 00:03:44.709
receives the boolean status.

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:47.502
Each call advances through the required input.

00:03:47.502 --> 00:03:50.052
The reader does not search for a name; its

00:03:50.052 --> 00:03:52.723
sequence and assignments give each recovered

00:03:52.723 --> 00:03:53.816
value its meaning.

00:03:53.858 --> 00:03:56.814
All required values are now available for the

00:03:56.814 --> 00:03:57.274
report.

00:03:57.274 --> 00:04:00.164
We will print each label beside its restored

00:04:00.164 --> 00:04:03.185
variable, then end the reader block and remove

00:04:03.185 --> 00:04:04.433
our temporary file.

00:04:26.622 --> 00:04:29.603
These labels are added to console output; they

00:04:29.603 --> 00:04:31.806
were not field labels in the file.

00:04:31.806 --> 00:04:34.463
The reader closes before finally performs

00:04:34.463 --> 00:04:35.047
deletion.

00:04:35.047 --> 00:04:37.768
Closing and deleting are separate actions.

00:04:37.768 --> 00:04:40.555
Finally also runs when the attempted record

00:04:40.555 --> 00:04:43.471
operations fail, although deletion can itself

00:04:43.471 --> 00:04:44.897
report an I/O failure.

00:04:44.933 --> 00:04:48.016
The complete program follows one field agreement

00:04:48.016 --> 00:04:49.879
from writing through reading.

00:04:49.879 --> 00:04:52.898
Let's click Run and inspect the actual restored

00:04:52.898 --> 00:04:53.283
offer.

00:04:58.653 --> 00:05:01.459
The report restores four seats, the price two

00:05:01.459 --> 00:05:03.829
point five, and true for booking open.

00:05:03.829 --> 00:05:06.386
Each typed read matches its write and its

00:05:06.386 --> 00:05:07.820
intended field meaning.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.564
The price is printed as a double value; this

00:05:10.564 --> 00:05:13.121
example does not add currency formatting.

00:05:13.121 --> 00:05:15.553
The following cleanup removes the file.

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:18.385
Now try a separate offer with seven seats, a

00:05:18.385 --> 00:05:21.218
price of three dollars and twenty-five cents,

00:05:21.218 --> 00:05:22.414
and booking closed.

00:05:22.414 --> 00:05:25.247
We will change only the three written values.

00:05:25.247 --> 00:05:27.765
The reader and all labels stay the same.

00:05:42.461 --> 00:05:45.437
Before running, predict every named output line.

00:05:45.437 --> 00:05:48.289
Match each read with the value written at that

00:05:48.289 --> 00:05:51.202
position, and explain how its assigned variable

00:05:51.202 --> 00:05:52.566
is used by the report.

00:05:52.566 --> 00:05:54.736
Pause here to make your prediction.

00:06:02.818 --> 00:06:05.770
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:06.693
the new record.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.537
The restored report shows seven seats, price

00:06:14.537 --> 00:06:17.337
three point two five, and false for open status.

00:06:17.337 --> 00:06:19.787
False is an actual value written into this

00:06:19.787 --> 00:06:20.720
complete record.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:23.520
It is different from omitting the required flag,

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.970
which would leave the boolean read without

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:26.728
enough input.

00:06:26.728 --> 00:06:29.412
Replaying the complete program creates another

00:06:29.412 --> 00:06:30.170
private file.

00:06:30.239 --> 00:06:32.970
You used typed output and input to recover the

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.642
event desk's offer with the same field types,

00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:36.830
order, and meanings.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.680
The notebook also shows why successful same-type

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.233
reads can assign wrong meanings, and why an

00:06:42.233 --> 00:06:45.083
incomplete required field is a separate failure.

00:06:45.083 --> 00:06:47.933
Those checks go beyond confirming that a program

00:06:47.933 --> 00:06:49.239
reached its last line.

00:06:49.304 --> 00:06:52.036
For your next step, imagine two integer fields

00:06:52.036 --> 00:06:54.769
for a room number and an available-seat count.

00:06:54.769 --> 00:06:57.442
What must the reader know beyond their shared

00:06:57.442 --> 00:06:59.164
type to label them correctly?

00:06:59.164 --> 00:07:01.719
Explain why a complete read alone would not

00:07:01.719 --> 00:07:04.154
prove that those meanings were preserved.
